\section{Introduction}

While the previous chapters have explored whether understanding of national and 
state level electoral activity can explain school board electoral activity, this 
chapter uses a different approach. I leverage a natural experiment created by 
the unprecedented policy shock of the passage of Act 10 in Wisconsin following the 
election of Governor Scott Walker to test whether or not local electoral activity 
can be influenced directly by state level policy. This question falls into a broader 
literature unpacking the federalist entanglements of the multi-level system of 
government in the US. The question of whether a policy shock at a higher level of 
government can influence electoral activity at lower levels of government is 
directly relevant to studies of the policy making process and the system of feedback 
and interaction between policy and electoral activity. 

There are two reasons to believe that the policy shock associated with the passage 
of Wisconsin Act 10 would lead to an increase in voter and candidate participation 
in school board elections. The first is that the political debate around Act 10, 
the historic protests, and the recall election campaign all increased the amount 
of information voters had about school boards, school board powers, and the 
positions of their local school boards. This increase in information decreased 
the costs associated with voting, and increased the ability of candidates to 
distinguish themselves (by taking a clear position on Act 10), making it more 
likely both voters and candidates would participate. 

Another mechanism comes from the new power granted to school boards. After Act 10 
boards were given the ability to unilaterally set compensation plans for their 
staff, which account for approximately 90\% of their annual operating costs. This 
new power strongly incentivized both employee unions and property owners to express 
their policy preference for school district expenditures to mobilize both candidates 
and voters. School boards now, more than ever, had the chance to actualize the 
preferences of voters either to hold down property taxes, or to protect school 
staff from budget cuts. 

What are the motivations of both candidates and voters that might be greater 
after this state level policy than before? For candidates, the door is opened 
for reformers who want to challenge status quo taxation and compensation policy. 
These candidates may seek to challenge current board members and capitalize on the 
increased public awareness about school funding and compensation plans to distinguish 
themselves from incumbents. Additionally, these candidates have more information 
about the likelihood of their success based on the implied or revealed preferences 
of the voters in their school district for Governor Walker -- both in the 2010 
Gubenatorial race and in the 2012 recall. 

For voters, the advantages are perhaps even more clear. Now voters have a high 
profile polarizing issue into which to divide candidates. This increases the 
information available for voters, decreasing the costs of voting. Additionally, 
if boards are given more power, voters have more reason to believe that their 
policy preferences can be actualized by electing a candidate more closely related 
to their preferences. 

It is important to stop and consider why this can be considered an exogenous 
shock, and thus allow for some causal inference. Figure \ref{fig:wiscotimeline2} 
shows the sequence of events in Wisconsin from 2011 to 2013. It shows that the 
2011 and 2012 spring elections were both post passage of Wisconsin Act 10. The 
2011 election was also marked by a highly contested statewide Supreme Court 
election and the ultimate effect of Act 10 was in doubt due to multiple legal 
challenges. By the 2012 election, Act 10 had been ruled constitutional and there 
was no state supreme court seat up for election. 

\clearpage
\begin{center}
\begin{figure}[h!]
\caption{Wisconsin Politics 2011-2013 Timeline}
\label{fig:wiscotimeline2}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth,height=1.75in]{img/wiscotimeline}
\end{figure}
\end{center}
\vspace{-48pt}

An additional advantage of this exogenous shock for the study of school board 
elections is that it creates three unique measures available statewide to peer 
into the preferences of communities. First, the level of polarization in the 
community in the recall election of Scott Walker. The result of this election can 
be seen largely as an opinion poll of voters on the issues of Act 10 and education 
funding in the state -- the two largest issues in the campaign.\footnote{While some have 
argued that another issue, the legitimacy of recalling the Governor in the first 
place was also important, this may also be a reflection on how strongly those 
voters felt about Act 10 in the first place.} All else equal, more polarized 
communities should exhibit greater contestation and turnout in school board 
elections due to the deeper conflict over the best way to manage public schools. 

A second measure that arises from this period is the level of turnout for the 
recall election itself. As with any election, voters without a strong preference 
either way were able to stay home. What's more, the election was at an unconventional 
time -- neither during the fall or the spring general election. Turnout was specifically 
driven by a desire to express support or opposition to the Governor. As such, 
it can be seen as an indication of the intensity with which a community was in 
favor of or against the policies of the Governor. \footnote{The recall election 
took place on June $5^{th}$, 2012. Turnout was 57.4\% with 2,516,065 voters 
casting votes for Governor out of a voting age population of 4,378,741. For more 
information see \url{http://gab.wi.gov/sites/default/files/Statewide\%20Percentage\%20Results_6.5.12\%20Recall\%20Election\_PRE\%20SEN21\%20RECOUNT.pdf}}

A final measure was created by the mechanics of Act 10 itself. Act 10 required 
public employee unions to recertify through an election process. Over the next 
two years all public employee unions in Wisconsin recertified. Recertification 
required unions to hold elections and that a majority of all members, not just 
of those who voted, must vote in favor of recertifying for the union to continue 
to operate. The results of these elections -- the margin by which the union 
recertified -- can be seen as a measure of latent union strength. While the 
vast majority of unions recertified, in many cases the elections were close. 

Unfortunately, the present study cannot distinguish between these two mechanism. 
More to the point, the study is limited by the reality that though school boards 
were given this freedom, it came coupled with historical cuts in state aid and 
a freeze on school district revenue limits. In practice this meant that though 
compensation policy may have been freed up, school boards had very little 
flexibility to innovate under such fiscal constraint. In some sense then, this 
leaves the elections post Act 10 as really a referendum on the opinion of voters 
and communities on both the changes to collective bargaining, but also reductions 
in school funding. 


\section{Theory}

The case of Wisconsin school boards can be framed two ways. First, school boards 
can be considered like any other elected office with elections that are responsive 
to the provision of information. Prior chapters have taken this approach. In those 
chapters, for the most part, I considered the school board election as an autonomous 
event without explicitly considering factors in neighboring local governments or 
state level policy directly. This follows somewhat the strategy of \citet{oliver12} 
in investigating local electoral influences. 

This approach, however, is not a complete picture. Special jurisdiction governments 
are indeed constrained by other governments. Legally, their existence is dependent 
upon authority granted by the state government. Fiscally, their existence is 
dependent on their ability to compete for tax revenues with other local governments. 

Another approach, however, is to view school boards as nested within a political 
system. When considering the influence of school boards on policy, it is common 
to consider them as constrained actors within a federal system. However, this 
same constraint is not accounted for when investigating the electoral behavior 
of these units. Within the larger literature on special district governments there 
is some consideration of the constraints of layered governments, but not particularly 
when it comes to electoral behavior. 

% Discussion of federalism, special district governance, and local elections


